alerts.action_en:
1) Run SELECT database, table, is_readonly, zookeeper_exception FROM system.replicas WHERE is_readonly to find the read-only tables and the reason;
2) Check that the ZooKeeper/Keeper cluster is healthy (echo ruok | nc <zk> 2181) and that ClickHouse can reach it;
3) Once ZooKeeper is back, run SYSTEM RESTART REPLICA <db>.<table> so the replica re-establishes its session;
4) If the metadata in ZooKeeper is lost, use SYSTEM RESTORE REPLICA to rebuild it, after confirming another healthy replica exists.

1) Run SELECT query, memory_usage, elapsed FROM system.processes ORDER BY memory_usage DESC LIMIT 5 to find the queries consuming memory, and KILL QUERY WHERE query_id='...' to stop the bleeding if needed;
2) Check the highest memory_usage entries in system.query_log to find SQL missing filters or doing large JOINs;
3) Use max_memory_usage / max_bytes_before_external_group_by to cap a single query and allow spilling to disk;
4) Confirm the mark cache and uncompressed cache are not configured too large.

1) Run SELECT name, free_space, total_space FROM system.disks to check each disk;
2) Run SELECT database, table, formatReadableSize(sum(bytes)) FROM system.parts WHERE active GROUP BY 1,2 ORDER BY sum(bytes) DESC to find the largest tables;
3) For tables with a TTL, run ALTER TABLE ... MATERIALIZE TTL to trigger cleanup, or DROP PARTITION to remove expired partitions;
4) Longer term, expand the disk or configure a multi-volume storage policy to move cold data down.

1) Run SELECT count(), user, client_hostname FROM system.processes GROUP BY 2,3 ORDER BY 1 DESC to see which clients the connections come from;
2) Compare with Query and TCPConnection in system.metrics to tell high query concurrency apart from a connection leak;
3) For clients that do not reuse connections, switch to a connection pool on the application side;
4) Check that max_concurrent_queries and max_connections match the instance size, and apply rate limiting if needed.

1) Run SELECT event_time, user, client_hostname, exception FROM system.query_log WHERE type='ExceptionBeforeStart' AND exception LIKE '%AUTHENTICATION_FAILED%' ORDER BY event_time DESC LIMIT 20 to find the source IP and username;
2) If it is your own application, verify the credentials in its config against users.xml;
3) If the source is an unknown IP, treat it as an intrusion attempt: tighten the networks allowlist in users.xml and rotate the password;
4) Confirm the ClickHouse port is not directly exposed to the internet.

